blem because you don't  need to maintain cell viability  and then the last potentially largest  advantage of  snook seek over a single serena seek is  that you don't need fresh specimens  so we're able to take archival frozen  specimens that were  stored for a different purpose many many  years ago and actually  obtain very high quality single nucleus  rna-seq data from them  at the present time so this workflow  summarizes uh to date the 27 specimens  that we have  uh processed about 50 are treatment  naive  and fifty percent uh have undergone  neoadjuvant chemo radiation and these  are all surgical specimens  that we're working with and we split the  specimens for snook seek and then a  subset for bulk rna-seq  um a multiplex ion beam imaging um as  well as  nanostring digital spatial profiling um  and what we've noticed empirically is  that  an rna integrity number um using the  agilent bioanalyzer  platform of greater than six seems to be  necessary in order to  have sufficient rna integrity for good  downstream snook seek  data so this slide summarizes the  15 patients in the treatment naive  cohort  you can see there's a broad range of  ages of genders  of a stage grade margin status a few of  them have the  more rare adnosquamas histology  and then also a pretty broad range in  terms of adjuvant treatment and clinical  outcomes  for the 12 neoadjuvant treated patients  we have a similar range  in terms of their clinical pathologic  features a couple of the patients had  brocco ii germline  mutations and all these patients got  neoadjuvant uh  radiotherapy the majority got falfirinox  uh as well um and then uh two patients  got nivolumab or losartan on trial  so we processed all these  these 27 specimens through our pipeline  and  we yielded after filtering quality  control  about 170 000 nuclei across the 27  patients  and we performed community detection  using a leiden algorithm  and we were able to identify distinct  cell populations  using known cell type specific gene  expression signatures  and you can see at the each of these  boxes is a umap for an individual  patient  naive on the left treated on the right  and the gray  cell clusters represent the tumor cells  the malignant cells  and then the various colored clusters  represent different  types of non-malignant cells including  acinar ductal endocrine endothelial  fibroblast immune  schwann and smooth muscle so all the  major cell types we were able to  identify with confidence  from the transcriptomic profiles in our  nuclei  on the bottom right those stack  proportion bar plots  are illustrating at an individual  patient level the wide range  of cell type distributions in the naive  and treated settings  so to further increase our confidence in  calling the malignant cells within our  tumors we performed inferred copy number  variation  analysis so this is just one example  patient and on the top  of this uh of this plot  basically across all the chromosomes  you're looking at the inferred copy  number variations by  a sliding window of 100 genes the  expression of 100 genes sliding along  each chromosome arm  and trying to see if they're  concordantly enriched or depleted  and inferring copy number variations  from that so on the top  this is from the normal adjacent  pancreatic tissue  from one of our tumors and you can see  there are really no  patterns suggesting cnb clonality  and then on the bottom this is the  actual tumor itself  so some are don't have any cmvs  and are likely non-malignant cell types  and then you can see  some distinct clusters marked in gray on  the left  that correspond to our malignant cells  where you see a very  distinct cnb pattern and then to  as a gut check we wanted to see since we  only had 27 patients  were the cmvs that we were inferring  from our 27 patients were those  representative pancreatic cancer  patients overall  so we for this we turn to uh the tcga  cohort of about 180 some patients  where they use snip and whole exome  sequencing to determine arm level  somatic copy number variations so  in the plot on the right i'm showing the  cnb proportion  relative to the chromosome arm and dark  blue is our snook seek data and light  blue is the tcga cohort and  in gray is a different cancer altogether  that's prostate cancer  so as you can see there is a pretty  close association between the  proportions  of cmvs that we saw in our data compared  to ccga  and very distinct from the prostate  cancer data from tcga  so the next thing we wanted to do was to  to really test this hypothesis that  snook c gives us better representation  of the cell type proportions present in  the actual  tumor in situ compared to the uh  single cell rna-seq approaches that have  been tried prior to this  so for this to give us our kind of  histologic ground truth if you will  we use multiplex ion beam imaging we use  the panel  of about 27 different targets but what  i'm showing you here is the main  compartments of interest  so the images on the top on the left in  red that's cd45  on the right is cd 31 and then green  in both cases is keratin dark blue is  vitamin  and then cyan is the nuclear compartment  marked by  a double-stranded dna antibody  and on the bottom four panels we used a  software package  called histacat um to basically  uh create uh to segment and create masks  for each of these compartments  and then from that we were able to  enumerate the number of cells  across three different fields of view  per specimen  and then when we average those together  and compared it with the  cell type enumeration by snoop seek you  can see for the eight patients that we  did this side-by-side comparison for  some differences but for the most part a  pretty close correlation between  mimi and snook seek for the proportion  of endothelial immune  fibroblasts and epithelial cells um and  then on the bottom right those are two  summary  stacked bar plots naive on the left  treated on the right for all the  patients combined and you can again see  a pretty close correlation so this  confirmed our hypothesis that  um the processing involved with snook c  seems to be able to better capture the  um the cell type proportions present in  these tumors  so next we combined all of our um 170  000 or so nuclei in the treated and  treatment naive cohorts and  made combined umaps out of them so gray  again is tumor  um is the malignant part of the tumor i  should say  and you can see that um the tumor cells  are segregating by patient um  as expected whereas the non-malignant  cell types are  grouping together across patients  and you can also make a few kind of  initial  qualitative observations here there's  significantly more  malignant cells in the naive cohort  compared to the treated and there seems  to be an enrichment for some of the  non-neoplastic cell types which  is better shown in these this box plot  here um the asana the ductal  the endocrine fibroblast compartments  are all  enriched in the post-treatment cohort  whereas the tumor is significantly  depleted as you would expect  with some treatment response and the  increase in  the asner ductal endocrine etc uh  cell cell counts  after treatment uh is thought to be both  related to potentially direct  stimulation and regeneration  of normal pancreatic tissue with  treatment but also  a a known desmoplastic contracture of  the tissue after chemo radiation  that just simply increases the density  of  some of these normal cell types that are  relatively resistant to chemotherapy and  radiation  so this plot um is just to illustrate  that you know without dwelling too much  on the specific details  that you can we can zoom in to you know  the immune population  endocrine population and use uh further  uh  uh  known cell type signatures to refine  these  uh larger groups into um various immune  cell types or endocrine  cell types and from a pure enumeration  sample we see some interesting changes  with treatment  for example in the plot on the left you  can see that there's a significant  depletion of t regs post treatment and  then on the right  i'm breaking out just the dendritic  cells in  the treatment naive setting on the left  and the treated setting on the right  and you can see that there's a  significant increase in the classical  dendritic cell type 1 or cdc ones  with treatment and a significant  depletion in cdc2s  and this is uh so much the case that  we were not even able to reliably detect  any cdc2s  in the post-treatment setting and this  is interesting because there's some  preliminary data  from uh some clinical trials looking at  neoadjuvant chemo radiation and the  effects on the immune system  that suggest uh this same shift towards  a cdc-1  dendritic cell phenotype which is  associated with uh cross-presentation to  cytotoxic t cells  and more of an anti-tumor effect  compared to cdc tubes which are known  for their role  in immunosuppression wound healing etc  so to go beyond enumeration and  look at in detail what changes are  occurring with  treatment we did a differential  expression analysis comparing  post-treatment  specimens with treatment naive specimens  so in these seven volcano plots here  the right side of the plot represents  genes that are enriched  in the post-treatment setting on the  left genes that are enriched in the  treatment naive setting  so this is still an ongoing analysis at  this time so i'll just call your  attention to a few highlights that  were very robust findings so on the top  left  in gray this is the neoplastic  compartment and we found that  chemo radiation was associated with an  enrichment in type 1 and type 2  interferon response genes  but also a depletion in developmental  programs  previously associated with the classical  progenitor subtype in bulk  which the compass trial recently found  uh was more sensitive to cytotoxic  therapy compared to the basal squamous  subtype so  our data seems to be consistent with  those findings  uh amongst the immune cells um i'll call  your attention to the cd8 t cells which  are shown in lavender  on the middle row the right volcano plot  um so the treatment was associated with  upregulation of several activation  programs  uh such as il-7r tcf7 stat 4 and slam f6  which have been uh linked to  memory activation cell survival as well  as  response to checkpoint inhibitors and  other cancers such as melanoma  and concordantly there was a depletion  in the exhaustion markers tim3 and cd39  which again in melanoma has been  associated with a lack of response to  checkpoint inhibitors  so this was interesting to us because  while  checkpoint inhibitors have largely  failed in metastatic  pancreatic cancer except in the in the  one percent of patients  that have microsatellite instability um  these results seem to suggest that  perhaps there's a different  situation when you combine  or combine immune checkpoint inhibitors  with chemo radiation in the neoadjuvant  setting and certainly that's something  that several trials are currently  looking into and will be interesting to  see what those results are  and then finally on the bottom right in  that yellow  volcano plot those are the fibroblasts  and  after treatment there seemed to be this  enrichment for  a hyper-activated myofibroblastic  phenotype  so the next thing we wanted to do was  you know use this opportunity  where we have this uh almost 170 000  nuclei from 27 patient samples  we could really use this opportunity to  kind of go beyond the  basal classical distinction that has  been well established in the bulk  setting  and look at what other common gene  expression  themes are shared across multiple  patients and might have some bearing on  the biological behavior of this very  aggressive tumor  so without going into some of the  specific details because  we actually uh recently did a  re-analysis  of this topic modeling i want to make  the point that  we do see when we do topic modeling  across our patients  an endoderm progenitor and an epithelial  topic very consistently  and those are very similar to the  classical  and basal squamous subtypes identified  in bulk  we also confirmed findings by other  groups that  the same tumor will express we'll have  cells that express a predominantly  classical profile whereas others will  express a predominantly basal profile  and those  are heterogeneous within the same tumor  but beyond that we see  other topics that are also very  prevalent  zankabal neural progenitor etc and we're  in the process  of doing this topic modeling not just  for the tumor cells but also the  fibroblasts and other cell types  and really trying to learn what themes  may correlate  or predict outcomes in this disease  and then taking the what we learn at the  single nucleus single cell level  and using that information to deconvolve  bulk rna-seq data  that's much more readily available and  translatable into  future clinical trials so this slide is  just showing  that if you classify a patient's  individual cells based on the dominantly  expressed  tumor or fibroblast topic you can see  there's a broad range  in terms of the heterogeneity and  distribution of different topics within  each patient tumor  so we were able to learn a decent amount  and we're still learning stuff  with further analyses in the  dissociative setting but we're really  limited by the fact that the tumor has  been you know has lost all spatial  information at this point  and a lot of the a lot of the important  interactions between different cell  types or even between the same cell  types  can only be captured if you have a  spatial fidelity and resolution  so we partnered with nanastring to  use their digital spatial profiling  platform  known as geomics to help us get  transcriptomic information  that's spatially resolved and really  take the data to another level  so what i'm showing here is a schematic  of the nanostring workflow  for their geomix platform and there's  two very similar paths you can take it's  just different the difference is just  the probe  you can go after protein targets or rna  we mainly focus on rna  i'm using ish probes but you could very  easily do the exact same things  with antibodies so these antibody or  rna-ish probes have been tagged with a  barcode  that has a uv cleavable  linker that attaches them to the probe  and we start with an ffpe slide  from the clinic and we do all of our  standard processing  d-waxing engine retrieval etc and then  once everything has been processed we  stain that slide  using a combination of four morphology  markers to label the nuclear compartment  and three other  morphological compartments of interest  and then a probe set from nanostring  that covers your targets of interest and  then after you label that  you put the slide onto the device and  there's a slide scanner built in  and you scan it and you see the four  color immunofluorescence image  and you're able to then go in and choose  specific regions of interest  or within those regions of interest  areas of interest that you want to  interrogate further  then the digital micro mirror device um  that nanostring developed will customize  the illumination mask to fit whatever  your region of interest or area of  interest looks like and it'll actually  move the  move to that area of the slide  illuminate and photo cleave  all those barcodes in that area of  interest and then  those will get sucked up by the straw  and deposited into a spatially indexed  96 well plate  and depending on what plex your panel is  you can either read out that spatially  indexed transcriptomic information  on the encounter platform or with ngs  so with nanostring we first started with  their  cancer transcriptome atlas or cta panel  this is a carefully curated uh over 1500  rna target panel that was collated from  various other  you know well-established cancer  signatures that are listed here  and here are three of our samples that  we have performed this protocol  on i'm showing side-by-side images of  the  four color amino fluorescence and the  rois that we selected  next to an h e image um  and what we've labeled in green the  epithelial compartment  in magenta the immune compartment and in  cyan  the fibroblast compartment  so the question that we were really  interested in answering um  that requires spatial resolution is  within the same tumor we expected that  there would be regions  that were infiltrated by immune cells  and regions that were  uh exclusionary to immune cells and that  there may be  tumor intrinsic or potentially  fibroblast intrinsic  transcriptional programs that were in  part governing this local  immune micro environment and we wanted  to understand what that was  with the hopes that that would lead to  improvements in  immunotherapy strategies in this disease  so what i'm showing here  is the three different types of regions  of interest that we see across  most of our patients on the left this is  an example of  tumor cells that are surrounded by  fibroblasts in cyan as well as immune  cells in magenta  the middle panel shows tumor cells that  are only surrounded by fibroblasts and  then on the right these are tumor cells  only surrounded by  immune cells and no fibroblasts so the  nanostring  geometrics platform has really nice auto  segmentation feature built in where you  can control  some of the parameters like thresholding  and erosion factors  but ultimately you get really nice  segmentation as you can see here  see here where we actually went after  very complex  patterns um you know based on what our  tumors look like  to actually separate out the tumor the  immune and the fibroblast compartments  and interrogate them separately  uh within each roi so we collected our  data  and uh we because this was over  800 plex we had an ngs readout and we  took that data the first thing we wanted  to do  was to see how it compared to the snook  seq data so we actually took  these snook seeks signatures for various  cell types and we use those to  deconvolve  the nanostring data as you can see in  this heat map here  we got a very clear epithelial cluster  on the top  the the green rois represent epithelial  and you can see it really matches the  tumor signature  from snook c similarly for calves this  would be one two three four the fifth  the fifth row down  you can see there's a very clear  fibroblast signature that correlates  between the nanostring  and the snook seq data and then various  immune populations also correlated  very well so this really demonstrated  nicely the coherence and complementarity  of the nanostring platform and the the  single nucleus rna-seq  approach so next we wanted to compare  the differential expression patterns in  the tumor and fibroblast compartments by  treatment  in the nanostring data compared to the  snook seek data so here you're seeing a  volcano plot  where genes enriched in treated  tumor aois are on the right and genes  enriched in naive tumor aois are on the  left  and without going into the details since  this is just a preliminary analysis i'm  showing you here  a lot of the initial genes that we're  seeing  are comparable between the two platforms  for both the tumor  and the fibroblasts which was very  reassuring to see  so moving into the spatial realm further  we used our data to ask the question  what genes are differentially expressed  in the tumor compartment  based on whether or not the roi is  infiltrated by immune cells or not  and we were able to  get pretty robust signatures from this  that we're still currently exploring  but this is data that we could only get  with spatial resolution and not with the  dissociated data  so we're really excited to explore this  further  and then we can also do something very  similar with the calf compartment  uh to try to derive fibroblast intrinsic  programs that may  govern the immune infiltration in the  local environment  so with topic modeling i mentioned  earlier that  we were able to move beyond the  traditional classical versus basal  distinction  and really look at a whole host  of themes that various tumor cells  within a  within a pancreatic cancer use to  potentially gain the fitness advantage  and  collaborate um for that goal and  from a very initial look you know we  only have 1500 or so genes in  in the cta which is a lot of genes  except when you're looking at the topics  those are derived from the whole  transcriptome so  quite a bit of imputation has to occur  because they're not all represented  in the cta panel but even with that  limitation  when you look for example across the  eight  topics that we identified in the cancer  cells  um we found some interesting  associations for example  the mesenchymal topic  was strongly associated with rois that  contained fibroblasts and then  looking at it in a different way we  found that the endoderm progenitor  um which is similar to the bulk  classical subtype  was associated with immune infiltration  more than other topics so these are  things that we're currently exploring  more  but certainly yielding some interesting  results already  so to address you know this limitation  as well as  um as well as really open the doors to a  lot of other potentially interesting  questions  nanostring has recently developed a  whole transcriptome  assay that is really  changing the way we're able to do this  analysis because we no longer have to  impute  certain genes in our topic signatures we  now have all the genes  available and we did a preliminary  analysis with them  that so far is showing some exciting  early results  so this is the four color  immunofluorescence from the  nanostring geomix platform and you can  see some example rois  that are that are overlaid on the image  and we found that across the  transcriptome we were able to  successfully detect 6600 genes spatially  with it with our samples and this is  without optimization just a very initial  pilot  and what i'm showing here is these in  these droid plots  the top 3 330  most highly expressed targets by segment  and the colors correspond to what we've  been using so cyan  represents the fibroblasts magenta is  the immune  and green is the epithelial and  to see if we're on the right track if  you zoom in on  one example fibroblast aoi  you can see that the scores after  deconvolving for our topics  are much higher for the fibroblast topic  compared to the tumor topics  for this fiberglass aoi and conversely  for the epithelial aoi  the tumor topics are much more highly  expressed in the fibroblast topics  so this was consistent you know with  what we would expect  and suggest that uh suggests that the  data is um  is is going to be very useful um as we  move forward  so we're very excited at this point you  know with the  preliminary data that we have that  between the  the better understanding of pancreatic  cancer molecular taxonomy  as well as more insights gained  looking at the effects of neoadjuvant  chemoradiation and other treatments  we're really going to be able to move  forward  with the spatially resolved technology  and start to understand the interplay  between  various components of these pancreatic  tumors and ultimately  be able to inform precision oncology and  pancreatic cancer  in a much more meaningful way so thank  you very much for  your attention i hope you found the  presentation useful  i'd like to thank several people without  whom none of this work would have been  possible  first and foremost my postdoctoral  advisors aviv regev  at the broad and tyler jacks at the koch  institute  um very close collaborators which who  have contributed to  every part of what i've shown you today  are karthik yagadish  a computational biologist in the regev  lab jimmy guo a ucsf md phd student  and hannah hoffman a research technician  working closely with me  i'd also like to express my gratitude to  my collaborators  at mgh brigham and dana farber  especially andy lists carlos fernandez  and mari mino  for the clinical side of this project  and then also my close collaborators at  nanastring um  dan zonger kit fuhrman and jason reeves  for all their help and then last but not  least um the funding agencies that have  made this work possible  um have been the lust garden foundation  and the  cancer foundation of asco thank you  you
 

